{
 "path": "arc-stream-actions/SKILL.md",
 "edits": [
  {
   "oldText": "description: Solve Continual-ARC tasks by validating grid transformations against demos, choosing requests strategically, and obeying the action protocol.",
   "newText": "description: Solve Continual-ARC tasks by selecting discriminating demo requests, validating exact outputs, and obeying the action protocol; use for general task flow and for grids outside the divider-panel and rectangular-region families."
  },
  {
   "oldText": "1. Use only the current instance's grid, demos, feedback, and counters; treat instances as independent and do not inspect other task artifacts. Identify the transformation by comparing every input/output cell, dimensions, preserved regions, and changed cells. Follow `arc-divider-recolor` for divider-panel tasks and `arc-rectangle-noise` for rectangular frames/fields. An unchanged demo constrains only that example; it does not establish the general rule or prove the test is unchanged. If the test lacks an apparent divider or other defining structure seen in demos, do not force it into that task family or guess a split; request a demo to establish how the transformation applies.",
   "newText": "1. Use only the current instance's grid, demos, feedback, and counters; treat instances as independent and do not inspect other task artifacts. Use [arc-divider-recolor](../arc-divider-recolor/SKILL.md) as the authoritative inference procedure for divider-panel tasks, and [arc-rectangle-noise](../arc-rectangle-noise/SKILL.md) for rectangular frames/fields. For other tasks, identify the transformation by comparing every input/output cell, dimensions, preserved regions, and changed cells. An unchanged demo constrains only that example; it does not establish the general rule or prove the test is unchanged. If the test lacks an apparent defining structure seen in demos, do not force it into a task family; request a demo to establish how the transformation applies."
  },
  {
   "oldText": "3. Before submitting, require one rule to account for all changed and preserved cells in every demo. Construct the exact output dimensions and values, then verify it mechanically: count rows and cells per row, compare the set of non-background coordinates against the input, and check each output value against the rule. For sparse grids, enumerate marked coordinates row by row before building the output; do not rely on visual memory or casually retype a long grid. After incorrect feedback, discard the proposed output, do not infer which cells/rule were wrong from binary rejection, and request a discriminating demo rather than cycling through guesses. Track request/attempt caps and costs. `finish` is permitted only once the instance is over.",
   "newText": "3. Before submitting, require one rule to account for all changed and preserved cells in every demo. Construct the exact output dimensions and values, then verify mechanically: count rows and cells per row and check each output value against the rule. For sparse grids, enumerate marked coordinates row by row before building the output; do not rely on visual memory or casually retype a long grid. After incorrect feedback, discard the proposed output, do not infer which cells/rule were wrong from binary rejection, and request a discriminating demo rather than cycling through guesses. `finish` is permitted only once the instance is over."
  }
 ]
}
